  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-10 md:py-14">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Jokhang Temple</h2>
      <p>The spiritual heart of Tibet -- and for many visitors, an even more powerful experience than Potala Palace. Built in the 7th century by King Songtsen Gampo, Jokhang houses the most sacred object in Tibetan Buddhism: a life-size gilded statue of the 12-year-old Buddha Shakyamuni, said to have been blessed by the Buddha himself. Thousands of pilgrims prostrate their way across Tibet to reach this statue.</p>
      <p>The atmosphere is unlike anything else: yak butter lamps flickering in the dim light, the low murmur of mantras echoing off ancient stone walls, monks in crimson robes moving through corridors worn smooth by centuries of devotion. The rooftop offers a panoramic view of the Potala Palace on the horizon and the Barkhor circuit below -- a perfect photo spot.</p>
      <p><strong>Tickets:</strong> CNY 85 | <strong>Hours:</strong> 7:00 AM - 5:30 PM | <strong>Time needed:</strong> 1-2 hours</p>

      <h2>Barkhor Street</h2>
      <p>The circumambulation circuit around Jokhang Temple, Barkhor Street is where Lhasa comes alive. Pilgrims walk clockwise, spinning handheld prayer wheels and murmuring mantras. Vendors sell prayer flags, turquoise jewelry, thangka paintings, Tibetan incense, and yak wool scarves from tiny stalls that have been in the same families for generations.</p>
      <p>Best visited in the late afternoon (4:00-6:00 PM) when the light turns golden, the crowds are at their peak energy, and the rooftop tea houses overlooking the circuit fill up with locals and travelers alike. Do not rush through Barkhor. Sit on the steps, watch the pilgrims, drink sweet tea. This is the real Lhasa.</p>
      <p><strong>Time needed:</strong> 1-3 hours (allow time for shopping and people-watching)</p>

      <h2>Sera Monastery</h2>
      <p>Sera is famous for one thing: the daily monk debates. Every afternoon (Monday-Saturday, 3:00-5:00 PM), monks gather in the tree-shaded debating courtyard for a ritual that has remained unchanged for centuries. The debates are philosophical -- monks question each other on Buddhist doctrine with dramatic gestures: the questioner claps his hands loudly, stamps his foot, and thrusts his arm forward to emphasize each point.</p>
      <p>Even without understanding Tibetan, the theater of it is mesmerizing. The sound of clapping hands echoing through the courtyard, the intensity of the exchanges, the younger monks nervously facing their seniors. Beyond the debates, Sera's main assembly hall contains beautiful murals and a serene atmosphere away from the tourist bustle of central Lhasa.</p>
      <p><strong>Tickets:</strong> CNY 50 | <strong>Time needed:</strong> 2 hours (arrive by 2:45 PM for the debates)</p>

      <h2>Norbulingka Palace</h2>
      <p>The summer palace of the Dalai Lamas, Norbulingka is a peaceful contrast to the formality of the Potala. Spread across 36 hectares of gardens, pavilions, and small palaces, it was built as a retreat -- a place for the Dalai Lamas to escape the summer heat. The main palace (Takten Migyur Potrang) was built by the 14th Dalai Lama in the 1950s and is the most recent addition.</p>
      <p>The gardens are lovely in May and June when the flowers are in bloom. Even in other months, the quiet pathways and shaded pavilions offer a welcome respite from Lhasa's intensity. The palace interiors are well-preserved and less crowded than Potala, giving you space to absorb the details.</p>
      <p><strong>Tickets:</strong> CNY 60 | <strong>Hours:</strong> 9:30 AM - 5:30 PM | <strong>Time needed:</strong> 1-2 hours</p>

      <h2>Beyond the City: Day Trips from Lhasa</h2>
      <h3>Drepung Monastery</h3>
      <p>Once the world's largest monastery, housing up to 10,000 monks at its peak. Located 8 km west of Lhasa at the foot of Mount Gephel. The main assembly hall is vast and atmospheric. Visit in the morning when monks perform their chanting rituals. <strong>Time needed:</strong> 2-3 hours, half-day trip.</p>

      <h3>Ganden Monastery</h3>
      <p>Perched on a mountain ridge 45 km east of Lhasa at 4,300 meters, Ganden offers some of the most dramatic monastery views in Tibet. The original monastery was largely destroyed but has been partially rebuilt. The kora (circumambulation path) around the monastery ridge takes about 1.5 hours and offers panoramic views of the Kyi-chu Valley. <strong>Time needed:</strong> Full day trip.</p>

