      <h2>Suggested 3-Day Lhasa Itinerary</h2>
      <p>Here is the most efficient route through Lhasa's top attractions, organized by geography and energy level. Each day builds on the last — start with lighter walking, end with the most demanding climbs.</p>

      <h3>Day 1: Acclimatization + Barkhor</h3>
      <p>Do not attempt anything physically demanding on your first day at altitude. Spend the morning resting at your hotel. In the late afternoon (after 3:00 PM), walk slowly to Jokhang Temple and spend an hour inside absorbing the atmosphere. Then join the pilgrims on the Barkhor circuit — walk clockwise, stop for sweet tea at a rooftop teahouse, and watch the city come alive. Dinner: try a Tibetan restaurant near Barkhor. This is a gentle, flat walk of about 2 km total. <strong>Total walking: 2-3 km, mostly flat. Altitude gain: minimal.</strong></p>

      <h3>Day 2: Potala Palace + Jokhang</h3>
      <p>Book the earliest Potala time slot (9:30 AM). The climb is steep — take it slowly and pause often. Allow 2-3 hours inside the palace. Exit through the west gate and walk to a nearby restaurant for lunch. In the afternoon, if energy permits, revisit Jokhang Temple or explore Barkhor's side streets and shops. You will have seen the two most important sites in Tibet in one day. <strong>Total walking: 4-5 km, with ~50m of steep stairs at Potala.</strong></p>

      <h3>Day 3: Sera Monastery + Norbulingka</h3>
      <p>Morning: Visit Norbulingka Palace (opens 9:30 AM). The flat, peaceful gardens are a welcome contrast to Potala's stairs. Spend 1-2 hours exploring the pavilions. Lunch near the palace. At 2:30 PM, take a taxi to Sera Monastery to secure a good spot for the 3:00 PM monk debates in the courtyard. After the debates, explore Sera's assembly halls. Evening: celebratory dinner at one of Lhasa's best Tibetan restaurants. <strong>Total walking: 3-4 km, mostly flat.</strong></p>

      <h3>If You Have a Fourth Day</h3>
      <p>Choose between Drepung Monastery (closer, half-day, morning chanting) or Ganden Monastery (farther, full-day, dramatic mountain setting). Both are worth the time. Drepung is easier logistically — a 20-minute taxi from central Lhasa. Ganden requires a 1-hour drive each way but rewards with extraordinary mountain monastery views.</p>

      <h2>Beyond the City: Day Trips from Lhasa</h2>
      <p>For day trips outside Lhasa, you will need a registered tour vehicle and guide. Independent travel outside the city is not permitted for foreign visitors. Your travel agency can arrange day trips as part of your tour package. Book at least one week in advance during peak season.</p>
      <p>One of Tibet's three sacred lakes, located 100 km southwest of Lhasa. The water is an extraordinary turquoise color that changes with the light. The best viewpoint is from the Kamba-la Pass (4,794m) on the road from Lhasa. <strong>Time needed:</strong> Full day trip. Note: this requires an Aliens' Travel Permit in addition to your TTP.</p>
